WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.400
So what if there were not only an internet of information,

00:04.400 --> 00:07.360
what if there were an internet of value?

00:07.360 --> 00:11.560
Some kind of vast, global, distributed ledger

00:11.560 --> 00:15.880
running on millions of computers and available to everybody,

00:15.880 --> 00:19.720
and where every kind of asset, from money to music,

00:19.720 --> 00:25.440
could be stored, moved, transacted, exchanged and managed,

00:25.440 --> 00:28.400
all without powerful intermediaries.

00:28.520 --> 00:33.280
What if there were a native medium for value?

00:33.280 --> 00:37.960
Well, in 2008, the financial industry crashed,

00:37.960 --> 00:43.640
and perhaps propitiously, an anonymous person or persons

00:43.640 --> 00:47.760
named Satoshi Nakamoto created a paper

00:47.760 --> 00:53.560
where he developed a protocol for a digital cash that

00:53.560 --> 00:57.440
used an underlying cryptocurrency called Bitcoin.

00:57.440 --> 01:01.680
And this cryptocurrency enabled people to establish trust

01:01.680 --> 01:05.000
and do transactions without a third party.

01:05.000 --> 01:07.480
And the seemingly simple act set off

01:07.480 --> 01:11.480
a spark that's ignited the world, that

01:11.480 --> 01:15.400
has everyone excited or terrified or otherwise

01:15.400 --> 01:17.680
interested in many places.

01:17.680 --> 01:19.640
Now don't be confused about Bitcoin.

01:19.640 --> 01:21.200
Bitcoin is an asset.

01:21.200 --> 01:23.000
It goes up and down.

01:23.000 --> 01:26.520
And that should be of interest to you if you're a speculator.

01:26.520 --> 01:28.840
More broadly, it's a cryptocurrency.

01:28.840 --> 01:32.360
It's not a fiat currency controlled by a nation's state.

01:32.360 --> 01:34.320
And that's of greater interest.

01:34.320 --> 01:37.560
But the real pony here is the underlying technology.

01:37.560 --> 01:40.640
It's called blockchain.

01:40.640 --> 01:44.080
So for the first time now in human history,

01:44.080 --> 01:47.760
people everywhere can trust each other and transact,

01:47.760 --> 01:50.000
peer to peer.

01:50.000 --> 01:54.280
And trust is established not by some big institution,

01:54.280 --> 01:57.320
but by collaboration, by cryptography,

01:57.320 --> 01:59.480
and by some clever code.

01:59.480 --> 02:03.080
And because trust is native to the technology,

02:03.080 --> 02:05.680
I call this the trust protocol.

02:05.680 --> 02:07.100
Now you're probably wondering, well,

02:07.100 --> 02:08.640
how does this thing work?

02:08.640 --> 02:11.280
Fair enough.

02:11.280 --> 02:15.160
Assets, digital assets like money to music and everything

02:15.160 --> 02:18.040
in between, are not stored in a central place.

02:18.040 --> 02:20.840
But they're distributed across a global ledger

02:20.840 --> 02:23.960
using the highest level of cryptography.

02:23.960 --> 02:26.600
And when a transaction is conducted,

02:26.600 --> 02:30.380
it's posted globally across millions and millions

02:30.380 --> 02:32.480
of computers.

02:32.480 --> 02:34.840
And out there around the world is a group

02:34.840 --> 02:36.640
of people called miners.

02:36.640 --> 02:37.760
These are not young people.

02:37.760 --> 02:40.200
They're Bitcoin miners.

02:40.200 --> 02:43.240
And they have massive computing power at their fingertips,

02:43.240 --> 02:48.600
10 to 100 times bigger than all of Google worldwide.

02:48.600 --> 02:51.120
And these miners do a lot of work.

02:51.120 --> 02:53.760
And every 10 minutes, kind of like the heartbeat

02:53.840 --> 02:57.080
of a network, a block gets created

02:57.080 --> 03:00.720
that has all the transactions from the previous 10 minutes.

03:00.720 --> 03:02.760
And then the miners get to work trying

03:02.760 --> 03:05.880
to solve some tough problems.

03:05.880 --> 03:07.240
And they compete.

03:07.240 --> 03:09.720
And the first miner to find out the truth

03:09.720 --> 03:13.800
and to validate the block is rewarded in digital currency,

03:13.800 --> 03:17.160
in the case of the Bitcoin blockchain with Bitcoin.

03:17.160 --> 03:20.200
And then, this is the key part, that block

03:20.200 --> 03:23.120
is linked to the previous block and the previous block

03:23.120 --> 03:26.480
to create a chain of blocks.

03:26.480 --> 03:28.280
And everyone is time stamped, kind of like

03:28.280 --> 03:30.440
with a digital wax seal.

03:30.440 --> 03:32.880
So if I wanted to go in and hack a block

03:32.880 --> 03:37.440
and say, pay you and you with the same money,

03:37.440 --> 03:39.960
I'd have to hack that block, plus all the preceding

03:39.960 --> 03:43.800
blocks, the entire history of commerce on that blockchain,

03:43.800 --> 03:47.360
not just on one computer, but across millions of computers,

03:47.360 --> 03:52.520
simultaneously, all using the highest levels of encryption

03:52.520 --> 03:54.440
in the light of the most powerful computing

03:54.440 --> 03:58.560
resource in the world that's watching me, tough to do.

03:58.560 --> 04:01.440
And this is infinitely more secure than the computer

04:01.440 --> 04:03.120
systems that we have today.

04:03.120 --> 04:06.600
Blockchain, that's how it works.

